\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LAW \'97 Canon of Symmetry (Non-Negotiable)
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 The Canon defines irreducible functional roles required for coherence: 
\f0\b Frame (Ethos), Gradient (Logos), Vector (Pathos)
\f1\b0 . The invariant preserved is 
\f0\b balance
\f1\b0 : no role may dominate, collapse into, or substitute for another. Balance is dynamic non-collapse under lawful motion, not equilibrium. Any system, interpretation, or action that violates this invariant fails structurally. This law does not command behavior; it constrains what 
\f2\i can coherently occur
\f1\i0 . It is always in force, regardless of domain, language, or intent.\

\f0\b POLICY \'97 Application Directive (Contextual & Optional)
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 Use the Canon as an instrument when interpreting, designing, or acting. For any task, explicitly identify the Frame (standpoint/scope), the Gradient (constraints, rules, invariants), and the Vector (directed action). Move only in ways that preserve balance. If the task cannot be completed without collapsing roles, halt and repair the structure. Policy may adapt to context, goals, or tooling, but it may never override or reinterpret the Law. Policy answers 
\f2\i how to proceed
\f1\i0 ; Law answers 
\f2\i what is allowed to proceed at all
\f1\i0 .\
